# callable-contracts-contravariant-rejected.bot -- tests/structural-contract-completions.test's
# scc-contravariant-rejected in a program whose erased-callable contract is
# what contravariant erasure puts there and nothing else.
#
# call1 calls its untyped parameter f. Given the structural type
# Fn{args: [Fn{args: [int], return: int, errors: [E1]}, int], return: any,
# errors: []}, call1 may be passed, through that type, a callable that fails
# E1, and its untyped f forgets that: contravariant erasure, which puts E1
# into the program's erased-callable contract -- what call1 is charged for
# calling f, which its contract of no error does not admit: Type where call1
# acquires the type (the field value), for E1. Before the boundary was
# checked the program was accepted and E1 escaped at run time.
#
# Why a suite of its own: callable-contracts.bot passes C1-raising callables
# into untyped parameters directly, so its erased-callable contract has C1
# whatever contravariant erasure adds or fails to add, and the test there
# could not tell whether contravariant erasure is counted at all
# (audit/structural-contracts/tools/mutants.txt's
# contravariant-erasure-dropped went unnoticed there). This program, like
# the Tcl test's, passes no callable to an untyped parameter except through
# that structural type. It is apart from callable-contracts-contravariant.bot
# (the admitted program) as well: were this program accepted, its driver
# go(Job {run: call1}, 1) would raise E1 at run time, which nothing
# handles, and abort the suite holding it -- this one, never the admitted
# program's.
#
# Imports: compiler and test (every suite).
import compiler
import test

error E1

# sccContravariant's Job with a contract of no error (ERRORS []).
struct Job:
    run: Fn{args: [Fn{args: [int], return: int, errors: [E1]}, int], return: any, errors: []}

fn register_scc_contravariant_rejected_tests():
    # scc-contravariant-rejected: call1 calls its untyped parameter; given
    # Fn{args: [Fn{... errors: [E1]}, int], errors: []} it is rejected (Type
    # at the field, for E1), where it used to escape at run time.
    fn contravariant_rejected() errors compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn raise1(x: int) -> int errors E1:
                if x > 0:
                    fail E1
                x
            fn call1(f, x):
                f(x)
            fn go(j: Job, k: int):
                (j.run)(raise1, k)
            go(Job {run: call1}, 1)
    test::rejects_at("scc-contravariant-rejected", compiler::DiagnosticCode::Type, 51, 26, contravariant_rejected)
    test::rejects_containing("scc-contravariant-rejected/identity", compiler::DiagnosticCode::Type, "declared error \"E1\"", contravariant_rejected)

with context test::suite()
register_scc_contravariant_rejected_tests()
test::report()
